---
import Article from '../layouts/Article.astro';
import FeeTable from '../components/FeeTable.astro';

const entryRows = [
  {
    service: 'Pre-Construction Audit',
    structure: 'Fixed fee, fixed scope. Budget, contract, schedule, and team reviewed before ground breaks.',
    fee: '$25,000 – $60,000',
    href: '/services/pre-construction-audit',
  },
  {
    service: 'Site & Cost Review',
    structure:
      'Fixed fee. A focused review of one question — a bid, a budget, a site condition, a contract draft — with a written opinion.',
    fee: 'from $15,000',
    href: '/project-review',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: [
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'How much does an owner’s representative cost?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'Our entry engagements are fixed-fee and published: the Pre-Construction Audit runs $25,000–$60,000 depending on project scale and documentation, and the Site & Cost Review starts at $15,000. Full representation is quoted per engagement, structured either as a percentage of construction cost that steps down as project size rises, or as a monthly retainer.',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Do you take referral fees or compensation from contractors?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'No. We accept no compensation from any party but the client — no referral fees, no rebates, no commissions, and no ownership interest in any contractor, supplier, or manufacturer.',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Why don’t you publish a price for full representation?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'Because the honest answer depends on scope: project complexity, duration, location, and how much of the consultant team is already in place. We publish the structure and the principles, and we quote the number after a scoping conversation — in writing, before you commit to anything.',
        },
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Article
  title="Fees — Erskine Advisory"
  heading="Fees"
  kicker="Published where we can be, principled where we can’t"
  description="Fixed, published fees for entry engagements. A clear structure — and firm refusals — for full owner’s representation. No compensation from anyone but the client."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>
    A firm whose entire argument is independence should not make you guess how it gets paid. Where an
    engagement has a fixed scope, we publish the fee. Where the scope is the project itself, we publish the
    structure and quote the number after we understand what we would be taking on — in writing, before you
    commit to anything.
  </p>

  <h2>Entry engagements — published</h2>
  <div class="not-prose mt-6">
    <FeeTable
      caption="Fixed-scope engagements. Final figure confirmed in the proposal, before any work begins."
      rows={entryRows}
    />
  </div>
  <p>
    The audit range reflects project scale and how much documentation exists to review. A project with a
    complete drawing set, a signed-ready contract, and a full consultant team sits at the lower end; a
    complex site with a partial team and an evolving budget sits at the upper. The proposal states the exact
    figure. It does not move afterwards.
  </p>

  <h2>Full representation — the structure</h2>
  <p>
    Full owner’s representation runs from design through warranty and is quoted per engagement. Two
    structures, chosen to fit the project:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Percentage of construction cost</strong>, stepping <em>down</em> as project value rises. The
      work of representing a project does not scale linearly with its budget, and the fee should not either.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Monthly retainer</strong>, for projects with long or uncertain timelines, so that our
      compensation is tied to time and attention rather than to the size of the number being spent.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Either way, the fee is agreed before the engagement starts, invoiced to the client directly, and never
    contingent on what the project spends.
  </p>

  <h2>What we refuse</h2>
  <p>These cost us nothing to publish, and no builder-affiliated firm can copy them:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No percentage-of-savings fees.</strong> A representative paid on “savings” has an incentive to manufacture them — usually out of your quality.</li>
    <li><strong>No referral fees, rebates, or commissions</strong> — from contractors, suppliers, manufacturers, or anyone else, in any direction.</li>
    <li><strong>No compensation from any party but the client.</strong> One paymaster, on purpose.</li>
    <li><strong>No interest in any contractor.</strong> We hold no ownership in, and take no payment from, any firm that could be engaged on your project.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A note on what fees buy</h2>
  <p>
    We will not tell you representation “pays for itself,” and we will never quote a percentage we supposedly
    save — any such number would be unverifiable, and this practice does not publish unverifiable numbers.
    What a fee buys is stated plainly on each service page: the documents reviewed, the site visits made, the
    reports delivered, and one party at the table with no position except yours.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/project-review">Request a project review</a> and we will respond with a scoped proposal — the
    fee stated, the deliverables named.
  </p>
</Article>
